("h3","Types of sentences in every tense"),
("ul",["**Affirmative**: *She plays tennis.*","**Negative**: *She does not play tennis.*","**Interrogative (Yes/No)**: *Does she play tennis?*","**Interrogative (Wh-)**: *When does she play tennis?*","**Negative interrogative**: *Doesn't she play tennis?* / *Why doesn't she play tennis?*"]),
("p","Contractions: *isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, haven't, hasn't, hadn't, won't (will not), shan't (shall not), don't, doesn't, didn't, can't, couldn't*. Third person singular: he, she, it, a singular noun (*Ravi, the dog, my mother*)."),
("h2","8.2 Present Tenses"),
("h3","1. Present Indefinite (Simple Present)"),
("p","**Form**: V1 (V1+s/es for he/she/it). Negative: do/does not + V1. Question: Do/Does + subject + V1?"),
("p","**Uses**: (1) habits and routines: *He gets up at five.* (2) universal truths and scientific facts: *The sun rises in the east. Water boils at 100°C.* (3) permanent situations: *My father works in a bank.* (4) timetables and schedules (future sense): *The train leaves at 6.* (5) commentary, stage directions, headlines: *Dhoni hits a six.* (6) in time/if-clauses for the future: *If it rains, we will stay.*"),
("p","**Key words**: always, never, usually, often, sometimes, daily, every day, generally, on Sundays, seldom, rarely."),
("table",["Type","Singular subject","Plural subject"],[
["Affirmative","She walks to school.","They walk to school."],["Negative","She does not walk.","They do not walk."],["Question","Does she walk?","Do they walk?"],["Wh-question","Where does she walk?","Where do they walk?"],["Negative question","Doesn't she walk?","Don't they walk?"]]),
("table",["Spelling of -s / -es","Examples"],[
["Add -s","walk→walks, sing→sings"],["Add -es after s, x, z, ch, sh, o","pass→passes, fix→fixes, watch→watches, wash→washes, go→goes, do→does"],["Consonant + y → -ies","study→studies, cry→cries"],["Vowel + y → -ys","play→plays, say→says"],["Irregular","have→has, be→is"]]),
("note","IMPORTANT","Do not add -s to the main verb when 'does' is used: *Does he **go**?* (not *goes*). *He doesn't **like** it.*"),
("h3","2. Present Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: am/is/are + V1+ing. **Uses**: (1) action happening now: *The baby is sleeping.* (2) action around the present time, temporary: *I am studying for the exams these days.* (3) planned future action: *We are leaving tomorrow.* (4) annoying habit with 'always': *He is always complaining.* (5) changing situations: *Prices are rising.*"),
("p","**Key words**: now, at present, at this moment, these days, nowadays, look!, listen!, today, currently."),
("table",["Type","Example"],[["Affirmative","I am reading."],["Negative","She is not (isn't) reading."],["Question","Are you reading?"],["Wh-question","What are you reading?"],["Negative question","Isn't he reading?"]]),
("p","Spelling of V+ing: drop final -e (*make→making*), double final consonant for short stressed verbs (*run→running, sit→sitting, begin→beginning*), -ie→-ying (*lie→lying, die→dying*). Stative verbs (know, want, like, belong) are not used in continuous."),
("h3","3. Present Perfect"),
("p","**Form**: have/has + V3. **Uses**: (1) an action just completed: *I have just finished my homework.* (2) a past action whose effect is visible now: *He has broken his arm.* (3) experience up to now: *Have you ever visited Agra?* (4) actions that began in the past and continue (with stative verbs, or since/for): *We have lived here since 2015.* (5) with unfinished time periods: *I have read three books this month.*"),
("p","**Key words**: just, already, yet, ever, never, so far, recently, lately, today/this week (unfinished), since, for."),
("note","IMPORTANT","The present perfect cannot be used with a definite past time: wrong *I have seen him yesterday.* → **I saw him yesterday.** Also wrong: *When have you come?* → **When did you come?** Use the simple past with: yesterday, last week, ago, in 2010, when."),
("table",["Word","Meaning and use","Example"],[
["just","a moment ago","She has just left."],["already","earlier than expected (affirmative)","I have already eaten."],["yet","until now (negative/question)","Have you finished yet? Not yet."],["ever / never","at any time / at no time up to now","Have you ever flown? I have never flown."],["so far","up to now","So far we have won two matches."]]),
("h3","4. Present Perfect Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: have/has + been + V1+ing. **Uses**: an action that began in the past and is still continuing, or has just stopped with visible result: *She has been studying since morning. It has been raining for two hours. Your eyes are red; have you been crying?*"),
("p","**Key words**: since, for, all day, how long, lately, recently."),
("table",["Word","Use","Examples"],[
["since","a point of time (starting point)","since 2010, since Monday, since morning, since he left, since I was a child"],["for","a period of time (duration)","for two hours, for ten years, for a long time, for ages"]]),
("note","IMPORTANT","Wrong: *I am living here since 2010.* → **I have been living / have lived here since 2010.** Never use the present continuous or simple present with 'since/for' for an action continuing up to now."),
("h2","8.3 Past Tenses"),
("h3","5. Past Indefinite (Simple Past)"),
("p","**Form**: V2; negative: did not + V1; question: Did + subject + V1? **Uses**: (1) completed actions at a definite time in the past: *I visited Udaipur last year.* (2) past habits: *He walked to school every day.* (3) a series of past actions in a story: *She opened the door, entered, and sat down.* (4) states in the past: *He was ill.*"),
("p","**Key words**: yesterday, last night/week/year, ago, in 2005, then, once, one day, when (a past event)."),
("note","IMPORTANT","After 'did / did not' use V1, never V2: *Did you **go**?* (not *went*); *I did not **see** him.* The past of 'be' is was (I/he/she/it) and were (you/we/they): *Was she there? They weren't happy.*"),
("h3","6. Past Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: was/were + V1+ing. **Uses**: (1) action in progress at a stated moment in the past: *At 8 p.m. last night I was watching TV.* (2) a long action interrupted by a short one: *I was crossing the road when a car stopped.* (3) two simultaneous past actions: *While she was cooking, he was laying the table.* (4) setting the scene in a story: *The wind was blowing and the rain was falling.*"),
("p","**Key words**: while, when, as, at that time, those days, all day yesterday."),
("h3","7. Past Perfect"),
("p","**Form**: had + V3. **Uses**: the earlier of two past actions (the 'past of the past'): *The patient had died before the doctor came. When I reached the station, the train had left.* Also in reported speech and in 3rd conditional."),
("p","**Key words**: before, after, by the time, when, as soon as, already, just, till, until."),
("table",["Earlier past (Past Perfect)","Later past (Simple Past)"],[["had finished","before she came"],["had left","when I reached"],["had already eaten","by the time we arrived"]]),
("note","NOTE","With 'after', either tense may be used: *After he had eaten, he left* = *After he ate, he left*. With 'before': *Before he left, he had locked the door.* The action that happened first takes the past perfect when the sequence is not obvious."),
("h3","8. Past Perfect Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: had + been + V1+ing. **Use**: an action that had been going on for some time before another past action or time: *She had been reading for two hours when I reached her house. He was tired because he had been working all day.*"),
("p","**Key words**: since, for, how long, all day, before."),
("h2","8.4 Future Tenses"),
("h3","9. Future Indefinite (Simple Future)"),
("p","**Form**: will/shall + V1. **Rule**: in strict grammar 'shall' goes with I/we, 'will' with you/he/she/it/they; to express **determination, promise, threat, command** the order reverses: *You shall have the prize* (promise); *I will not forgive him* (determination); *He shall pay for this* (threat). Today 'will' is used with all persons in ordinary statements. Negative: will not (won't) + V1. Question: Will/Shall + subject + V1?"),
("p","**Uses**: (1) simple prediction or fact about the future: *It will rain tomorrow.* (2) decision made at the moment of speaking: *I'll take this one.* (3) promises, offers, requests: *I will help you. Will you open the door?* (4) with 'think/hope/expect/probably'."),
("p","**Key words**: tomorrow, next week/year, soon, tonight, in a few days, by and by, one day."),
("h3","10. Future Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: will/shall + be + V1+ing. **Uses**: an action in progress at a stated time in the future: *At this time tomorrow, she will be writing her exam.* Also polite enquiry: *Will you be using the car tonight?*"),
("h3","11. Future Perfect"),
("p","**Form**: will/shall + have + V3. **Use**: an action that will be completed before a certain time in the future: *I will have finished the project by Saturday.* **Key words**: by, by the time, before, by then, by next month."),
("h3","12. Future Perfect Continuous"),
("p","**Form**: will/shall + have + been + V1+ing. **Use**: duration of an action up to a point in the future: *By next June, she will have been teaching here for ten years.* **Key words**: for, since, by next year, when…."),
("h2","8.5 Ways to Express Future Time"),
("table",["Way","Use","Example"],[
["will/shall + V1","prediction, decision, promise","I will go to Agra tomorrow."],
["Present simple","timetable / schedule","What time does the match begin? The train leaves at 5."],
["Present continuous","fixed arrangement","The Prime Minister is leaving for America next week."],
["be going to + V1","intention or sure prediction from evidence","I am going to take a bath. Look at the clouds; it is going to rain."],
["be about to + V1","immediate future","The train is about to arrive."],
["be to + V1","official plan, command","The President is to address the nation."]]),
("h2","8.6 Tenses in Clauses: Sequence of Tenses"),
("ol",["If the main clause is in the **present or future**, the subordinate clause can be in any tense suitable for its meaning: *He says that he is busy / was busy / will come.*","If the main clause is in the **past**, the subordinate clause is normally in a **past form**: *He said that he was busy. I knew that she had left.*","Exception: **universal truths** stay in the present: *The teacher said that the earth moves round the sun.*","In **time clauses and 'if' clauses** about the future, use the present, not 'will': *When he comes, I will tell him.* Not *When he will come…*; *If it rains, we will stay.*","With **before/after** in the past: *He had finished before I arrived.*","**Would** is the past of will: *I thought he would come.*","**Comparisons with than/as** allow any tense: *He is taller than I was last year.*"]),
("h2","8.7 Tense Contrast and Common Confusions"),
("table",["Pair","Difference","Example"],[
["Simple past vs Present perfect","Definite finished time vs connection with now","I lost my pen yesterday. / I have lost my pen (I cannot find it now)."],
["Present perfect vs Present perfect continuous","Result/completion vs duration/activity","I have painted the room (it's done). / I have been painting the room (all day)."],
["Past continuous vs Simple past","Action in progress vs completed action","I was reading when he came. / I read the book last week."],
["Past perfect vs Simple past","Earlier action vs later action","The show had begun when we reached."],
["Present simple vs Present continuous","Habit/fact vs now","She sings well. / She is singing now."]]),
("note","IMPORTANT","Common errors: (1) *He is working here since 2012* → **has been working**. (2) *I have seen the film yesterday* → **saw**. (3) *If it will rain, we will stay* → **If it rains**. (4) *She told that she is ill* (past report) → **that she was ill**. (5) *He didn't went* → **didn't go**. (6) *The train has left before I reached* → **had left**. (7) *I am knowing him* → **I know him**. (8) *When did you come? I have come yesterday.* → **I came yesterday.**"),
